WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.571
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:09.037
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:17.550
Unit thirty-one: Information, Knowledge and Wisdom. In this unit, you will argue persuasively about information in the digital age.

4
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:19.981
data deluge.

5
00:00:20.681 --> 00:00:25.234
data deluge means an overwhelming flood of digital information.

6
00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:29.624
In the data deluge, attention becomes the scarcest resource.

7
00:00:30.324 --> 00:00:33.127
Your turn. Say: data deluge.

8
00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:37.153
data deluge.

9
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.650
knowledge management.

10
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:46.506
knowledge management means the organised way an institution collects, shares and uses what it knows.

11
00:00:46.806 --> 00:00:51.163
Good knowledge management stops a school losing experience when teachers leave.

12
00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:55.095
Your turn. Say: knowledge management.

13
00:00:57.795 --> 00:00:59.458
knowledge management.

14
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.078
wisdom of crowds.

15
00:01:02.778 --> 00:01:09.387
wisdom of crowds means the idea that a large group's combined judgement is often better than an individual's.

16
00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:13.393
The wisdom of crowds works only when people judge independently.

17
00:01:14.093 --> 00:01:17.082
Your turn. Say: wisdom of crowds.

18
00:01:19.782 --> 00:01:21.213
wisdom of crowds.

19
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:23.543
expert knowledge.

20
00:01:24.243 --> 00:01:29.818
expert knowledge means deep, specialised understanding gained through study and experience.

21
00:01:30.118 --> 00:01:34.115
Expert knowledge is not the same as a confident online video.

22
00:01:34.815 --> 00:01:38.233
Your turn. Say: expert knowledge.

23
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.248
expert knowledge.

24
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.913
information asymmetry.

25
00:01:45.613 --> 00:01:51.246
information asymmetry means a situation where one side knows much more than the other.

26
00:01:51.546 --> 00:01:55.984
Information asymmetry lets dishonest sellers cheat buyers.

27
00:01:56.684 --> 00:01:59.894
Your turn. Say: information asymmetry.

28
00:02:02.594 --> 00:02:04.256
information asymmetry.

29
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.774
tacit expertise.

30
00:02:07.474 --> 00:02:12.991
tacit expertise means skill that experts have but find hard to put into words.

31
00:02:13.291 --> 00:02:17.229
A master weaver's tacit expertise cannot be learned from a manual.

32
00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:21.650
Your turn. Say: tacit expertise.

33
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.989
tacit expertise.

34
00:02:26.983 --> 00:02:28.101
knowledge gap.

35
00:02:28.801 --> 00:02:34.597
knowledge gap means a difference in knowledge between groups, often widened by unequal access.

36
00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:37.964
Rural internet access could narrow the knowledge gap.

37
00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:41.978
Your turn. Say: knowledge gap.

38
00:02:44.678 --> 00:02:45.772
knowledge gap.

39
00:02:46.783 --> 00:02:48.841
signal and noise.

40
00:02:49.541 --> 00:02:55.325
signal and noise means useful information versus irrelevant or misleading information.

41
00:02:55.625 --> 00:02:58.763
Good analysts separate signal and noise.

42
00:02:59.463 --> 00:03:02.812
Your turn. Say: signal and noise.

43
00:03:05.512 --> 00:03:06.965
signal and noise.

44
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.135
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:10.435 --> 00:03:14.632
Ms. Sarah, we have more information than ever. Are we wiser?

46
00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:19.962
Are we? We are drowning in data and starving for wisdom.

47
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.840
But the wisdom of crowds says many people together are smart.

48
00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:32.288
Sometimes. Crowds can be wise, and crowds can be mad. Which crowd are you trusting?

49
00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:35.989
So expert knowledge still matters?

50
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:43.003
Matters? It is essential. Information is not knowledge, and knowledge is not wisdom.

51
00:03:44.317 --> 00:03:47.907
Then the real skill is separating signal and noise.

52
00:03:49.217 --> 00:03:56.188
Exactly. Filter, verify, reflect: that is the discipline of a thinking mind.

53
00:03:57.483 --> 00:04:02.060
Grammar focus: Rhetorical devices in argument (extended).

54
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.138
Rhetorical devices make arguments memorable.

55
00:04:05.438 --> 00:04:12.154
We are drowning in data and starving for wisdom. Information is not knowledge, and knowledge is not wisdom.

56
00:04:12.454 --> 00:04:15.103
Filter, verify, reflect.

57
00:04:15.403 --> 00:04:23.742
Rhetorical questions, antithesis and the rule of three make arguments memorable; combine them with evidence, not instead of it.

58
00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.983
Now read and listen. From Data to Wisdom.

59
00:04:28.283 --> 00:04:34.009
Every day, humanity produces an almost unimaginable quantity of data.

60
00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:39.394
Yet the data deluge has not obviously made us wiser.

61
00:04:40.683 --> 00:04:50.787
Thinkers often describe a hierarchy: data becomes information, information becomes knowledge, and knowledge, with judgement, becomes wisdom.

62
00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:58.971
Organisations use knowledge management to capture what they know, though tacit expertise is notoriously hard to record.

63
00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:06.555
The wisdom of crowds can outperform individuals, but only when opinions are diverse and independent.

64
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:16.618
Meanwhile, information asymmetry gives power to those who know more, and the knowledge gap between connected and unconnected communities widens.

65
00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:22.830
In such a world, expert knowledge and careful reasoning remain indispensable.

66
00:05:24.117 --> 00:05:29.750
The essential skill of our age is not finding information but separating signal and noise.

67
00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:34.027
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:34.327 --> 00:05:38.370
A situation where one side knows much more than the other is blank.

69
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:45.633
A, information asymmetry. B, knowledge management. C, wisdom of crowds.

70
00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:52.928
The answer is A: information asymmetry.

71
00:05:54.417 --> 00:05:59.365
'We are drowning in data and starving for wisdom' is an example of blank.

72
00:05:59.665 --> 00:06:05.432
A, antithesis. B, understatement. C, ellipsis.

73
00:06:09.417 --> 00:06:12.124
The answer is A: antithesis.

74
00:06:13.617 --> 00:06:16.487
'Filter, verify, reflect' uses blank.

75
00:06:16.787 --> 00:06:21.892
A, a tricolon. B, a passive. C, a conditional.

76
00:06:25.883 --> 00:06:28.347
The answer is A: a tricolon.

77
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.755
Skill that is hard to put into words is blank.

78
00:06:33.055 --> 00:06:39.286
A, tacit expertise. B, signal and noise. C, data deluge.

79
00:06:43.283 --> 00:06:46.548
The answer is A: tacit expertise.

80
00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:51.675
According to the text, when does the wisdom of crowds work?

81
00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:58.275
A, when opinions are diverse and independent. B, when experts lead. C, always.

82
00:07:02.283 --> 00:07:06.616
The answer is A: when opinions are diverse and independent.

83
00:07:08.117 --> 00:07:13.962
Now it's your turn. Part one: Does the internet make young people wiser or only better informed?

84
00:07:19.262 --> 00:07:25.905
Part two: Use a rhetorical question, an antithesis and a tricolon in your answer.

85
00:07:32.205 --> 00:07:41.957
Are we wiser because we can search anything? Not necessarily. We have more information but less attention. To become wise, we must read, question and reflect.

86
00:07:42.257 --> 00:07:44.186
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:45.183 --> 00:07:51.249
Great work today! You learned eight new words and rhetorical devices in argument (extended).

88
00:07:51.549 --> 00:07:56.175
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:59.383
See you in Unit thirty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:59.683 --> 00:08:02.173
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

